      "claim": "The sustained sniping and shelling campaign constitutes a war crime under Article 8(2)(b)(i) of the Rome Statute because the attacks were intentionally directed at the civilian population as such.",
      "reasoning": "Article 8(2)(b)(i) criminalises \"Intentionally directing attacks against the civilian population as such or against individual civilians not taking direct part in hostilities\". The factual record shows that the attacks were aimed at civilians queuing for water, attending funerals and crossing intersections, with no identifiable military objective and no discernible military advantage, demonstrating the requisite intent to target civilians. Accordingly, the conduct satisfies the elements of the war‑crime provision.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "rome_statute",
          "article": "8(2)(b)(i)",
          "quoted_text": "Intentionally directing attacks against the civilian population as such or against individual civilians not taking direct part in hostilities;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The campaign violated Article 51(2) of Additional Protocol I, which prohibits acts whose primary purpose is to spread terror among the civilian population.",
      "reasoning": "Article 51(2) states that \"Acts or threats of violence the primary purpose of which is to spread terror among the civilian population are prohibited.\" The pattern of attacks—continuous, indiscriminate, and directed at civilians engaged in ordinary daily activities—demonstrates that the primary purpose was to terrorise the population rather than to achieve a concrete military objective. Hence the conduct breaches this explicit prohibition.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "51(2)",
          "quoted_text": "Acts or threats of violence the primary purpose of which is to spread terror among the civilian population are prohibited."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The attacks were indiscriminate violations of Article 51(4) and Article 57 of Additional Protocol I, because they were not directed at specific military objectives and the expected civilian harm was excessive relative to any concrete and direct military advantage.",
      "reasoning": "Article 51(4) prohibits indiscriminate attacks, defining them as attacks \"not directed at a specific military objective\" and those that \"are of a nature to strike military objectives and civilians or civilian objects without distinction.\" The factual matrix shows that many of the sniping and shelling incidents occurred where no military target was present. Moreover, Article 57(2)(a)(iii) requires commanders to refrain from attacks that would cause excessive incidental civilian loss compared with the anticipated military advantage. The campaign, lacking any discernible military advantage, failed this proportionality test, rendering the attacks unlawful.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "51",
          "quoted_text": "Indiscriminate attacks are prohibited. Indiscriminate attacks are: (a) those which are not directed at a specific military objective; (b) those which employ a method or means of combat which cannot be directed at a specific military objective; or (c) those which employ a method or means of combat the effects of which cannot be limited as required by this Protocol; and consequently, in each such case, are of a nature to strike military objectives and civilians or civilian objects without distinction."
        },
        {
          "type": "treaty",
          "instrument": "ap_i",
          "article": "57",
          "quoted_text": "refrain from deciding to launch any attack which may be expected to cause incidental loss of civilian life, injury to civilians, damage to civilian objects, or a combination thereof, which would be excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated;"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The accused bears command responsibility for the war crimes because he ordered, facilitated, and encouraged the campaign while possessing effective control over the forces and receiving regular reports on the attacks.",
      "reasoning": "Customary international law imposes liability on superiors who, knowing or should have known about the commission of war crimes by subordinates, fail to prevent them or punish the perpetrators. The facts establish that the accused exercised effective command, issued orders that promoted the campaign, and was kept informed of its execution, satisfying the elements of command responsibility. Consequently, he is individually criminally liable for the violations identified above.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The conduct also breaches Article 85(3)(b) of Additional Protocol I, which criminalises the launching of indiscriminate attacks in the knowledge that they will cause excessive civilian harm.",
      "reasoning": "Article 85(3)(b) expressly penalises \"launching an indiscriminate attack affecting the civilian population or civilian objects in the knowledge that such attack will cause excessive loss of life, injury to civilians or damage to civilian objects, as defined in Article 57, paragraph 2 (a) (iii).\" The campaign’s documented intent to terrorise civilians, combined with the absence of military advantage, demonstrates that the perpetrators acted with the requisite knowledge of excessive civilian harm, thereby violating this provision.",
      "citations": [
